{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.QJucysMurphyEigen","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.QJucysMurphyEigen","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.QJucysMurphy","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.HoefsmitRepGeneral","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SytRowSuperstandard"],"declarations":21,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.barSymJMSum_block_eigenvalue_zero_false","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.barSymJMSum_block_eigenvalue_zero_false","kind":"theorem","line":430,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L430-L462","doc":"**The `N = 0` case is false — recorded, not silently excluded.**\n\n`Θ` at `n = 0` is `L_0 + L_0⁻¹ = 1 + 1`, which every algebra map sends to `2 · 1`, while the empty\npartition's `cellContentCoshSum` is the empty sum `0`.  Since `d_λ ≥ 1` always\n(`instNonemptyStandardYoungTableau`), the matrix algebra is nontrivial and `2 · 1 ≠ 0`.\n\nHence the `0 < N` hypothesis on `barSymJMSum_block_eigenvalue_of_pos` is not an artefact of the proof\nroute: the statement without it is refutable.\n\n**`(2 : K) ≠ 0` is a real hypothesis, not bookkeeping.**  Over `ℝ` the contradiction was\n`1 + 1 = 0` in the block algebra, closed by `simp` on a diagonal entry.  In characteristic\ntwo that equation *holds*, so this statement is **false** there and the refutation needs the\ncharacteristic assumption.  It is the one place in the general-field tranche (bean\n`qou-j377`) where the migration is not a pure retyping.  The mismatch is a **normalisation** one — `Θ` counts\n`k = 0,…,n` (that is `n+1 = N` Murphy elements, one per cell, correct for `N ≥ 1`), and at `N = 0`\nthe `ℕ`-truncated `N - 1 = 0` makes the range one term long against zero cells. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.barSymJMSum_block_eigenvalue_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.barSymJMSum_block_eigenvalue_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":401,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L401-L428","doc":"**Block eigenvalue of `Θ` on the irreducible `ρ_λ` — PROVED, for `0 < N`.**\n\nFor every `λ ⊢ N` with `N ≥ 1` the seminormal representation `ρ_λ : H_{N-1}(q) →ₐ M_{d_λ}(ℝ)`\nsends the central bar-symmetrised Murphy sum `Θ = Σ_k (L_k + L_k⁻¹)` to the scalar matrix\n`(Σ_{c∈λ} (q^{2c} + q^{−2c}))·1 = cellContentCoshSum λ q · 1`.\n\nThe two ingredients: `seminormalRep_qJM` (Ram (1997) Thm 4.1 — `L_k` is diagonal with entry\n`q^{2·content}` at the cell holding `k+1`) and `sum_cellOf_range` (summing over `k` sweeps every\ncell once, so the diagonal is constant).\n\n`q ≠ 0` with `q ^ 2 ≠ 1` says over `ℝ` exactly that `q` is not a root of unity — the genericity\n`[ram1997]` presupposes, and what `seminormalRep` takes.  It is **weaker** than the `0 < q ∧ q ≠ 1`\nthe `sorry`'d version asked for: negative `q` is admitted here.\n\nRefs: [ram1997] Theorem 4.1; [hoefsmit1974]; [murphy1992]. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_barSymJMSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_barSymJMSum","kind":"theorem","line":362,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L362-L390","doc":"**The bar-symmetrised Murphy sum acts by the cell-content scalar.**  Each `L_k + L_k⁻¹`\ncontributes `q^{2c} + q^{−2c}` at the cell holding the value `k+1`; summing over `k = 0,…,n` sweeps\nevery cell of `μ` once (`sum_cellOf_range`), so the resulting diagonal is *constant* — i.e. a\nscalar matrix, with the scalar `Σ_{c∈μ} (q^{2c} + q^{−2c})`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.sum_cellOf_range","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.sum_cellOf_range","kind":"theorem","line":335,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L335-L360","doc":"**Reindexing over a tableau's values.**  `k ↦ cellOf T (k+1)` is a bijection\n`range (n+1) → μ.cells` — its inverse is `c ↦ T.entry c − 1` — because the entry map of a standard\ntableau is a bijection from the cells onto `{1,…,|μ|}`.  So a sum indexed by the values is a sum\nover the cells, and in particular is independent of `T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_qJMInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_qJMInv","kind":"theorem","line":309,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L309-L326","doc":"**The inverse Murphy element acts by the inverse eigenvalue.**  Derived from the two-sided\ninverse `qJM · qJMInv = 1 = qJMInv · qJM` plus uniqueness of inverses, so it needs no second\ninduction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagValInv_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagValInv_mul","kind":"theorem","line":301,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L301-L307","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_mul_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_mul_inv","kind":"theorem","line":293,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L293-L299","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagValInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagValInv","kind":"def","line":288,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L288-L291","doc":"The inverse eigenvalue matrix: diagonal with `q^{-2·content}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_qJM","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_qJM","kind":"theorem","line":269,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L269-L286","doc":"**Ram (1997) Thm 4.1, for this construction.**  The `q`-Jucys–Murphy element `L_k` acts on\nthe seminormal representation as the diagonal matrix reading `q^{2·content}` at the cell holding\nvalue `k+1`.\n\nInduction on `k`: the base is `L₀ = 1` with value `1` at the corner (`jmDiagVal_one`), and the\nstep is the conjugation identity `genMat_conj_jmDiagVal`, since `L_{k+1} = σ_k L_k σ_k`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:content-scaling-correspondence","label":"prop:content-scaling-correspondence","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/content-scaling-correspondence.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_conj_jmDiagVal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_conj_jmDiagVal","kind":"theorem","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L177-L267","doc":"**The inductive step, as a matrix identity.**  Conjugating the eigenvalue matrix at value\n`k` by the generator matrix `genMat q k` gives the eigenvalue matrix at value `k+1`.\n\nEvery entry reduces to `Seminormal.jmStep_diag` / `jmStep_offDiag` (size-`2` orbits) or\n`Seminormal.seminormalParam_sq_mul` (size-`1` orbits). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.cellContent_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.cellContent_succ","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L170-L175","doc":"The content advances by the axial distance: `content(cell of k+1) = content(cell of k) + d`.\nImmediate from the definition of `axialDist` as a content difference. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_one","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L158-L168","doc":"**Base case.**  Value `1` sits at `(0,0)` in every standard tableau\n(`TableauCells.cellOf_one`), whose content is `0`, so the eigenvalue matrix is the identity —\nmatching `ρ(L₀) = ρ(1) = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L154-L156","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_diag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal_diag","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L150-L152","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.jmDiagVal","kind":"def","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L144-L148","doc":"**The `q`-JM eigenvalue matrix at value `v`**: diagonal, with `q^{2·content}` read off the\ncell holding `v` in each tableau. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.jmStep_diag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.jmStep_diag","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L121-L131","doc":"**Diagonal entry of the step is the shifted content.**  `a(d)²q^{2c}` plus the\noff-diagonal round trip equals `q^{2(c+d)}` exactly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.jmStep_offDiag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.jmStep_offDiag","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L113-L118","doc":"**Off-diagonal entry of the step vanishes.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_sq_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_sq_mul","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L89-L97","doc":"The inadmissible branch in one statement: for `d = ±1`, `a(d)² · q^{2c} = q^{2(c+d)}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_sq_of_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_sq_of_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L83-L87","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_sq_of_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_sq_of_one","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L74-L81","doc":"**Size-`1` orbit: the scalar squares to the content shift.**  At axial distance `±1` the\ngenerator acts by the scalar `a(±1)` (`q` resp. `-q⁻¹`), and its square is `q^{2d}` — exactly the\nfactor by which `q^{2·content}` changes when the content moves by `d`.  This is the inadmissible\nbranch of the inductive step. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_weighted_cancel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.seminormalParam_weighted_cancel","kind":"theorem","line":37,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/QJucysMurphyEigen.lean#L37-L72","doc":"**The weighted cancellation behind the JM eigenvalue.**  Weighting the two seminormal\nparameters of a size-`2` orbit by the `q^{2·content}` of their own cells makes them cancel:\n\n  `a(d)·q^{2c} + a(-d)·q^{2(c+d)} = 0`.\n\nThis is exactly the off-diagonal of `S · D · S` on that orbit, so it is what keeps the conjugate\nof a diagonal matrix diagonal — the inductive step of Ram's Theorem 4.1. ","signatureChunk":0}]}